---
import { getCollection } from 'astro:content';
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import CaseStudyCard from '../components/CaseStudyCard.astro';
import { site, conversationMailto, contractMailto } from '../data/site.ts';
import { careerStartYear } from '../data/experience.ts';

const studies = (await getCollection('caseStudies', ({ data }) => !data.draft)).sort(
  (a, b) => a.data.order - b.data.order,
);

const notes = (await getCollection('notes', ({ data }) => !data.draft)).sort(
  (a, b) => a.data.order - b.data.order,
);

const startYear = careerStartYear();
---

<Base title={`${site.name} — ${site.role}`} description={site.description} bareTitle>
  <section class="mx-auto max-w-wide px-5 pt-20 pb-16 sm:px-8 sm:pt-28 sm:pb-20">
    <p class="eyebrow">{site.role} · Systems that hold money and time · Remote</p>

    <h1 class="mt-5 max-w-3xl text-4xl leading-[1.1] sm:text-5xl lg:text-6xl">
      I build production systems where being wrong costs somebody money.
    </h1>

    <div class="mt-8 max-w-2xl space-y-5 text-lg text-muted">
      <p>
        Backend engineering since {startYear}. Most of it agency work on e-commerce platforms —
        high-traffic stores processing transactions and customer data, including Dyson’s. The
        kind of systems where an outage means lost orders rather than a failed build. Four of
        those years were on day rate, including a booking-and-payments platform I designed,
        built and then ran for the same client for four years.
      </p>
      <p>
        Then I stopped. Six years travelling, March 2020 to March 2026, no software in any of
        them. What brought me back was coding agents — I could suddenly build the things I had
        only ever been able to spec. What I build and operate now is a live scheduling, payments
        and video platform — web and Android, Next.js, React Native, Postgres, Stripe Connect
        with cross-border payouts — carrying real bookings and real money.
      </p>
      <p>
        I bring the delivery pipeline with me: a merge gate that has to be green before anything
        lands, a coverage floor that only ratchets upward, migration-drift and deployment
        checks, and the decision records that say why each of them exists. The two systems
        written up below are described with their trade-offs and mistakes intact, not as a
        portfolio list.
      </p>
      <p>
        And I build almost entirely with coding agents — more than 8 in 10 commits across those
        systems were written or co-written by an agent, counted from git history, not estimated.
        The same sessions provision the servers, run the deploys and maintain the decision
        records the next session reads. It is mostly a story about verification rather than
        generation: what I built is the system they operate inside.{' '}
        <a href="/work/agent-operated-codebase/" class="link-underline text-accent">
          How that works
        </a> is stated plainly rather than left to be inferred.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-10 flex flex-wrap gap-3">
      <a href="/contact/" class="btn btn-primary">Available for contract work</a>
      <a href="/work/agent-operated-codebase/" class="btn btn-secondary">Read the case study</a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section
    aria-labelledby="evidence-heading"
    class="border-y border-line bg-band py-16 sm:py-20"
  >
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-wide px-5 sm:px-8">
      <p class="eyebrow">The evidence</p>
      <h2 id="evidence-heading" class="mt-3 max-w-2xl text-3xl">
        Claims about AI-native engineering are cheap. These are counted.
      </h2>
      <p class="mt-4 max-w-2xl text-muted">
        Everything this site argues rests on artifacts you can inspect: figures counted from git
        history by script, a practice written up with its failures included, and a tool you can
        run yourself. Where a number is an estimate, it says so.
      </p>

      <dl class="mt-10 grid gap-x-10 gap-y-6 sm:grid-cols-3">
        <div>
          <dt class="eyebrow">The practice</dt>
          <dd class="mt-1.5 text-muted">
            Two production systems, more than 8 in 10 commits written or co-written by an agent,
            run solo inside verification gates —{' '}
            <a href="/work/agent-operated-codebase/" class="link-underline">the case study</a>,
            including where it rotted.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt class="eyebrow">The tool</dt>
          <dd class="mt-1.5 text-muted">
            groundtruth — an open-source CLI and GitHub Action that fails CI when an agent
            instruction goes false. Born from{' '}
            <a href="/notes/a-compiler-for-agent-context/" class="link-underline">
              auditing my own context layer
            </a>; it gates this site’s own pull requests.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt class="eyebrow">The measurements</dt>
          {
            /*
            A start month, never a duration. "Five months" was both stale on
            arrival and wrong: the earliest of the two production repositories
            was created on 2026-04-16 and the second on 2026-05-01, so every
            commit the metrics count postdates April 2026 — the March restart
            is when the work resumed, not when the counted history begins.
            A duration also invites a reader to do the arithmetic against
            today's date and find it does not add up; a start month is
            self-updating and cannot be argued with. Same rule the spoken
            version follows (delivery-co, conversation-ladder.md).
          */
          }
          <dd class="mt-1.5 text-muted">
            Delivery metrics from every agent-operated commit since April 2026 — authorship,
            rework, cycle time, cost — publishing to{' '}
            <a href="/notes/" class="link-underline">the notes</a> as they land.
          </dd>
        </div>
      </dl>

      <p class="mt-10 max-w-2xl text-muted">
        One operator’s systems are a starting point, not an answer. The point of measuring mine
        honestly is to have something worth comparing with yours.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section
    aria-labelledby="work-heading"
    class="mx-auto max-w-wide px-5 py-16 sm:px-8 sm:py-20"
  >
    <h2 id="work-heading" class="text-3xl">Selected work</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 max-w-2xl text-muted">
      The agent-operated practice this work runs on, and the two products it ships — one in
      production carrying real payments, one built and parked pre-launch. All described with
      their limitations intact.
    </p>

    <div class="mt-10 grid gap-6 lg:grid-cols-2">
      {studies.map((entry) => <CaseStudyCard entry={entry} />)}
    </div>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="notes-heading" class="border-y border-line bg-band py-16 sm:py-20">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-wide px-5 sm:px-8">
      <div class="flex flex-wrap items-end justify-between gap-4">
        <div>
          <h2 id="notes-heading" class="text-3xl">Notes</h2>
          <p class="mt-3 max-w-xl text-muted">Shorter pieces on specific problems.</p>
        </div>
        <a href="/notes/" class="link-underline text-sm text-accent">All notes →</a>
      </div>

      <ul class="notes-list mt-10 divide-y divide-line border-y border-line">
        {
          notes.map((note) => (
            <li class="group relative py-5">
              <span class="font-mono text-xs text-muted">{note.data.topic}</span>
              <h3 class="text-lg">
                <a
                  href={`/notes/${note.id}/`}
                  class="after:absolute after:inset-0 after:content-[''] group-hover:text-accent"
                >
                  {note.data.title}
                </a>
              </h3>
            </li>
          ))
        }
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section
    aria-labelledby="contact-heading"
    class="mx-auto max-w-wide px-5 py-16 sm:px-8 sm:py-20"
  >
    <h2 id="contact-heading" class="max-w-2xl text-3xl">
      I am available for a three-to-six-month contract.
    </h2>
    <p class="mt-4 max-w-2xl text-muted">
      Senior TypeScript, Next.js and Postgres — most useful where the hard part is the system
      rather than the screen, and where I would be reviewing other people’s code as well as
      writing my own. I contracted for four years before the break, on two concurrent
      engagements, so this is a return rather than a first attempt.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-4 max-w-2xl text-muted">
      UK-based and available now. Currently on a temporary working trip abroad, with a daily
      overlap from about 13:00 UK and flexibility outside it. Back in the UK early in the
      engagement, and on site from then.
    </p>

    <div class="mt-8 flex flex-wrap gap-3">
      <a href={contractMailto} class="btn btn-primary">{site.email}</a>
      <a href="/contact/" class="btn btn-secondary">Other ways to reach me</a>
    </div>

    <p class="mt-10 max-w-2xl text-sm text-muted">
      And if your team builds with agents, I want to hear how it is really going — twenty
      minutes, nothing sold, notes compared both ways.{' '}
      <a href={conversationMailto} class="link-underline">That conversation starts here</a>.
    </p>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  /* The topic column is sized from the longest topic on the page, not to a
     fixed width.

     It was `w-28`, and `topic` is an unbounded string in the content schema:
     "Working with agents" needed about 8.5rem, wrapped inside 7rem, and made
     that row a line taller than its neighbours so the three titles no longer
     shared a rhythm. Any width picked by hand is one topic away from the same
     bug, so the column measures itself instead — `max-content` on the list,
     `subgrid` on each row, giving every row one shared column exactly as wide
     as the longest topic needs.

     Only from 640px up. Below that there is not enough width for two columns,
     and the row falls back to the stacked block layout it already used when
     the old flex row wrapped. Browsers without `subgrid` get that same stacked
     row, which is a degradation rather than a break. */
  @media (min-width: 640px) {
    .notes-list {
      display: grid;
      grid-template-columns: max-content 1fr;
    }

    .notes-list > li {
      display: grid;
      grid-column: 1 / -1;
      grid-template-columns: subgrid;
      align-items: baseline;
      column-gap: 1.5rem;
    }

    .notes-list > li > span {
      margin-bottom: 0;
    }
  }

  .notes-list > li > span {
    display: block;
    margin-bottom: 0.25rem;
  }
</style>
